= Solution
{parent=ii}

The image contains $t$ and $a^2$, hence also $ta^2t^{-1}=a^3$. It therefore contains $a=a^3(a^2)^{-1}$, so it contains both generators of $S$. More explicitly,
$$
\boxed{\theta(tat^{-1}a^{-1})=a,\qquad\theta(t)=t,}
$$
which proves surjectivity.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the <commutator> $c=[a,tat^{-1}]$, the image is
$$
\boxed{\theta(c)=[a^2,ta^2t^{-1}]=[a^2,a^3]=1.}
$$
The last equality holds because two powers of the same element commute.

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Expand the <commutator>:
$$
c=at a t^{-1}a^{-1}t a^{-1}t^{-1}.
$$
Its possible pinches have intervening coefficients $a$, $a^{-1}$, and $a^{-1}$. For a $t(\cdot)t^{-1}$ pinch the coefficient must lie in $\langle a^2\rangle$; neither $a$ nor $a^{-1}$ does. For the $t^{-1}(\cdot)t$ pinch it must lie in $\langle a^3\rangle$, and again $a^{-1}$ does not. Here $\langle a\rangle\cong\mathbb Z$ embeds by the <normal form theorem for an HNN extension>, so these divisibility tests are valid inside $S$.

The word is a <reduced sequence in an HNN extension> with four stable letters. <Britton's lemma> gives
$$
\boxed{c\ne1,\qquad c\in\ker\theta.}
$$
Thus $S$ is a finitely presented <non-Hopfian group>: it has a surjective <endomorphism> which is not injective. This group is the <Baumslag-Solitar group> $BS(2,3)$.

